{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact20b23e82013a2e53c2f56aeb42ebf987d2294042/tree99b62f940adfc1fa4e6e203c90f88fca898725f0, primary and sole artifact notes/2026-10-15-directory-partitions.md read in full. The versioned root/partition split is useful, but the following paths prevent approval.\n\nF1 P1 — Reserve handoff capacity before index traffic can exhaust it (4.5 lines140-145, 4.7 lines187-195). Crossing SEAL_AT only blocks new opening acts; index deliveries remain admissible and no seal is scheduled automatically. An allowed execution delivers enough existing-lane updates before anyone signs seal to consume ordinary admission capacity. The signed seal then needs an entry plus the duties its tell creates; none was reserved. The root still holds the old partition active, so open-partition is blocked by P1. A1 only drops index at a full scope and cannot perform the missing seal. A quarter-budget target is not a handoff guarantee. Specify a reservation or equivalent admission invariant that protects seal, its diagnosis/result and recovery while index deliveries and outstanding settlements continue. Include a small-budget witness that delays the signer past sustained index traffic and still completes exactly one handoff.\n\nF2 P1 — Preserve control and authority reads after sealing (4.3, 4.5 lines141-143). The note says the current helpers work unchanged if the control item is called repository. Source directory.ts:400 selects repository in state open only; directoryMembership and directoryRulesScope at411-421, and repositoryAt at447-451, depend on that selector. Changing that item's state to sealed makes membership/rules disappear and makes index-number's repositoryAt fail. The promised retry-seal grant and post-seal indexing cannot work as written. Keep a stable control state with a separate admission flag, or explicitly version all affected selectors and subject bindings to cover sealed. Witness authorized retry, post-seal existing/new concern index and late creation settlement across restart.\n\nF3 P1 — Recover successor creation when it has no result (4.2 applied/refused/conflict clauses; section6). A root can send create-partition, receive routing refusals and record an undelivered diagnosis. Its item remains creating because no undelivered clause or root retry act is defined. open-partition refuses while any creating item exists. Mixed unanswered/routing attempts instead produce delivery-unavailable with no clause, and dispatch stops; that case is also unhandled. Source settle.ts:49-73 distinguishes those findings, and delivery.ts:283-284 ignores late results after undelivered. Define recovery for both, retaining original seed/cause/incarnation and reconciling an authentic late result without making two live successors. Do not treat delivery-unavailable as proof that no child exists. Cover pure routing failure and lost-answer/mixed failure separately.\n\nF4 P2 — Budget recorded failures as well as successful generations (section3.2, section7 lines237-241 and the root-growth witness). At most two entries per generation holds only for the normal success path. The note itself allows an original seal and retry-seal to both arrive: the second is a distinct request and its refusal consumes a root entry. derive/delivery.ts:165-178 writes deciding entries for refused requests; exact retransmission deduplication does not deduplicate two distinct sends. A late applied creation in another incarnation can also record conflict after the first result. Diagnoses and additional recoveries need entries too. Replace the unconditional 20,000-entry lifetime claim and witness with an explicit successful-work bound plus bounded or truthfully capacity-limited failure/control histories, retaining space for compromised relay and required settlements. If control traffic can exhaust the root early, state the supported envelope and failure behavior.\n\nF5 P2 — Q4 retains the point-lookup bottleneck that c9a2c676 required removing (section5 lines204-205; Q4 lines284 and307-309). Dividing the number identifies the generation, but finding it still walks the root list and finding its row still walks that partition. rowOf also scans all lane rows for each index update. A partition can gain concern rows after seal, so SEAL_AT does not even bound its final row count. A maximum lifetime row count is not the requested row/work-bounded point lookup. Define direct generation/number-to-row and lane-to-row access, with a stated per-request row/byte/work limit and no collect-all fallback. An explicit mapping/item identity or separately reviewed index contract is preferable to silently weakening the accepted consumer requirement. Witness a point lookup and index update beside many unrelated rows.\n\nF6 P2 — Remove the 300-second completion promise (section6 lines231-233). dispatchRetryMaxSeconds is the maximum delay between attempts, not a deadline for successful creation. outbox.ts:137-139 caps each delay; retryable unavailable answers can continue indefinitely, and a diagnosis may require authorized recovery. State the temporary no-active-partition condition and observable pending/recovery outcomes without claiming bounded completion under outage.\n\nQ decisions: Q2 number jumps, Q3 sparse bounded pages and Q5 grant reuse are acceptable. Q6/Q7 require the promised root-versus-partition consumer and real authority witnesses before Source; F2 is already a concrete compatibility failure. Q8's quarter threshold is a tuning choice only after F1's invariant. Q9 must use the same fully specified failure/recovery protocol.\n\nQ1: stopping endless advisory resends is reasonable, but A1 needs an explicit versioned delivery/transport/attempt-log contract before Source. Preserve source/send/address/handler verification and restrict terminal drop to the declared advisory handler and permanent admission exhaustion. The reader must distinguish an incomplete index, including a concern whose first row was dropped, from complete authoritative lane coverage. Head alone is insufficient if reserved-entry admission refuses before literal scopeEntries. No applied/recorded fact may be invented for dropped data.\n\nArchitecture: preserve old pins, immutable partition identity, generation ranges and per-partition head qualification; resolve F1-F5 before dependent Source. Security: retain current membership grants and authenticate all terminal-drop and root handoff paths; never replace an ambiguous creation with a second unaccounted active child. Simplification: reuse existing indexed pages and stable control records, while making recovery and point lookup explicit rather than adding whole-history scans.\n\nStatic design review only. No Source edit, test, gate, provider operation, push or deployment. The candidate differs from Main0548 only by this design note. Tests and source are unchanged; no repeated gate is needed. This is neither Source approval nor authorization to run the proposed protocol.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d6c57d7714a1130880dd730ecef78a594c775272","binding":"assigned","head":"20b23e82013a2e53c2f56aeb42ebf987d2294042","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5b04a11ca12c9932a809422efaf5fbb194e84aaa\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:66bca0c253653385aa5325636d8de9481f07306a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2b9b955b7acf08adb0b5f7e285e71c837f64cc5a","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}